:spcBef><a:spcPts val="480"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="–"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" cap="none" i="0" lang="en-US" strike="noStrike" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>release peptide and tRNA from ribosome</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr algn="l" indent="-285750" lvl="1" marL="742950" marR="0" rtl="0"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="480"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="–"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" cap="none" i="0" lang="en-US" strike="noStrike" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>cause subunits of ribosome to dissociate so that initiation can begin again</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:cxnSp><p:nvCxnSpPr><p:cNvPr id="525" name="Google Shape;525;p73"/><p:cNvCxnSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvCxnSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="304800" y="1371600"/><a:ext cx="8382000" cy="0"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="straightConnector1"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln cap="flat" cmpd="thinThick" w="57150"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:prstDash val="solid"/><a:miter lim="800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" type="none" w="med"/><a:tailEnd len="med" type="none" w="med"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="Termination of protein synthesis in bacteria. 
This image begins with the same large and small subunits bonded together with the scalloped cutouts. There is only one elongated intertwined green structure. This one has several blue balls bonded together on top and traveling upwards and out of the subunit. The mRNA line originates with 5[prime] and ends with 3[prime}. It has also has a break just after it starts. A pink box on this line is parallel with the right side of the scalloped right column in the small subunit. To the right is a purple puzzle piece labeled as RF, Release factor binds. This has an arrow pointing to the right column of the small subunit. This also indicates that the release factor binds. Below this is a down arrow indicating the peptidyl-tRNA link is hydrolyzed. This points down a replica of the first image, but the there are no blue balls. These are labeled as polypeptide and are outside the image on the left, and bonds right to COO-. This image also has the purple RF image sitting right to the elongated intertwined green structure just above the UAG in a pink box on the green line. Below this is a long down arrow which has the GTP image and a blue circle with IF3 joining it. On the left is a curved arrow pointing from RRF to the EF-G image bonding right to GDP [plus] Pi. An arrow juts left to RRF. Another arrow juts right to the elongated intertwined green structure and the RF structure. Below this is the mRNA line that previously traveled through the small subunit, but this is a straight horizontal line. Below this is the yellow large subunit on the left (50S) and to the right is the smaller subunit (30S), with the IF3 circle on the top left. 
